        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>The third-smallest constellation can be found partway along one of the imaginary lines of the summer triangle</p><p>We are on the hunt for another small, northern summer constellation this week: Sagitta, the arrow. Although it consists of just a handful of faint stars, Sagitta’s distinctive shape (it really does look like an arrow) makes it easy to identify against a dark sky.</p><p>The chart shows the view looking up towards the zenith while facing south from London at 22.30 BST on Monday.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/aug/31/starwatch-sagittas-arrow-shape-makes-it-easy-to-see-against-a-dark-sky\">Continue reading...</a>"
